<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the standard enthalpy change (ΔH°) for the reaction, we can use the following formula:

ΔH° = Σ [n × ΔH°f(products)] - Σ [n × ΔH°f(reactants)]

where ΔH°f is the standard enthalpy of formation, and n is the standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard standard stoichiometric coefficient.

In this reaction, the reaction is:

MnO(s) + H2O(l) → Mg(OH)2(s)

The enthalpy of formation for the reaction is:

ΔH° = [1 × ΔH°f(Cl2(g))] - [1 × ΔH°f(Ag(s)) + 1 × ΔH°f(Br2(l)))]

ΔH° = [1 × (-986.2 kJ/mol)] - [1 × (-635.1 kJ/mol) + 1 × (-285.8 kJ/mol)]

ΔH° = -986.2 kJ/mol - [-635.1 kJ/mol - 285.8 kJ/mol]

ΔH° = -986.2 kJ/mol - (-920.9 kJ/mol)

ΔH° = -986.2 kJ/mol + 920.9 kJ/mol

ΔH° = -65.3 kJ/mol

Therefore, the standard enthalpy change for the reaction is -65.3 kJ/mol.
The correct answer is \(\boxed{-65.3 \text{ kJ/mol}}\).
