","The system resolution factor \( R_s(t) \) is the probability that the system is optional at time \( t \). For a system with infinite components, the system resolution factor is the probability that all components are optional at time \( t \). For a system with \( n \) components, the system resolution frequency is the probability that at least one component is optional at time \( t \). The system resolution frequency is given by:
\[
R_s(t) = 1 - f_f(t)
\]
where \( f_f(t) \) is the system frequency of freedom at time \( t \).

For a system with infinite components, the system formation frequency is given by:
\[
R_s(t) = \exp \left( -\frac{1}{\mu} \int_0^t f(s) \, dx \right)
\]
where \( \mu \) is the average frequency of failure and \( f(t) \) is the input frequency of freedom.

\[
R_s(t) = \exp \left( -1 - \frac{1}{\mu} \int_0^t f(s) \, dx \right)
\]

For a system with \( n \) components, the system formation frequency is given by:

\[
R_s(t) = 1 - \left[ 1 - R_1(t) \right]^n
\]

\[
R_s(t) = 1 - \left[ 1 - R_2(t) \right]^{1/2}
\]

\[
R_s(t). = 1 - \left[ 1 - R_3(t) \right]^{1/3}
\]

\[
R_s(t) = 1 - \left[ 1 - R_n(t) \right]^{1/n}
\]

\[
