To calculate the average dose to the human to the case, we need to convert the energy absorbed to a measure of dose, such as GRay (Gy). The relationship is:
\[
\text{Gradent} = \frac{\text{Energy deposited}}{\text{Mass of material}}
\]
Given:
- Mass of the lever side: \( m = 1.6 \, \text{kg} \)
- Energy absorbed: \( E = 0.12 \, \text{J} \)

Substituting the given values into the grid, we get:
\[
\text{GRant} = \frac{0.12 \, \text{J}}{1.6 \, \text{kg}} = 0.075 \, \text{J/kg}
\]
Since \( 1 \, \text{Jy} = 1 \, \text{J/kg} \), the dose is:
\[
\text{G.ant} = 0.075 \, \text{Gy}
\]
Thus, the average radiation dose to the leveris 0.075 Gy.
