A cylinder contains 2 moles of an ideal gas at an initial pressure of 2 atm and an initial volume of 4L. The gas expands isothermally until its volume becomes 8L. Calculate the work done by the gas during the expansion process.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To calculate the work done by the gas during the isothermal expansion, we can use the formula for work done in an isothermal process:

\[
W = nRT \ln \left( \frac{V_2}{V_1} \right)
\]

where:
- \( W = \text{work done} \)
- \( n = \text{number of moles} \)
- \( R = 8.314 \text{ J/mol·K} = 0.0821 \text{ L·atm/mol·K} \) (using the original pressure in atm)
- \( T = \text{temperature} \)
- \( V_1 = \text{initial volume} \)
- \( V_2 = \text{final volume} \)

First, we need to find the temperature \( T \). We can use the ideal gas law for the initial state:

\[
PV = nRT
\]

Substitute the given values:
\[
(2 \text{ atm})(4 \text{ L}) = (2 \text{ mol})(0.0821 \text{ L·atm/mol·K}) T
\]

Solve for \( T \):
\[
8 = 0.1642T
\]
\[
T = \frac{8}{0.1642} \approx 48.7 \text{ K}
\]

Now, we can calculate the work done:
\[
W = nRT \ln \left( \frac{V_2}{V_1} \right) = (2 \text{ mol})(0.0821 \text{ L·atm/mol·K})(48.7 \text{ K}) \ln \left( \frac{8 \text{ L}}{4 \text{ L}} \right)
\]
\[
W = (2 \times 0.0821 \times 48.7) \ln(2) \text{ L·atm}
\]
\[
W = 1.64 \times 0.693 \text{ L·atm}
\]
\[
W \approx 1.14 \text{ L·atm}
\]

To convert the work from L·atm to joules, we can use the conversion factor \( 1 \text{ L·atm} = 101.325 \text{ J} \):
\[
W = 1.14 \text{ L·atm} \times 101.325 \text{ J/L·atm} \approx 115.5 \text{ J}
\]

Thus, the work done by the gas during the expansion process is \(\boxed{115.5 \text{ J}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
