","To determine the percentage of NO₂ molecules in the gas, we use the vant heat formula for vapour density:

\[
\text{Vapour density} = \frac{\text{Molecular mass}}{\text{Relative molecular mass}}
\]

Given that the vapour density of N₂O₄ is 25.67 at 100°C and 760 mm Hg, we can write:

\[
\text{Vapour density} = \frac{\text{Mass of 1 mole of N}_2\text{O}_4}{\text{Mass of 1 mole of N}_2\text{O}_2}
\]

The dissociation of N₂O₄ is:  
\[
\text{N}_2\text{O}_4 \rightarrow 2\text{NO}_2
\]

Let \( x \) be the moles of N₂O₄ and \( y \) be the moles of NO₂. The total number of moles in the gas is \( x + 2y \).

The average molecular mass of the gas is:
\[
\text{Average molecular mass} = \frac{\text{Mass of } \text{N}_2\text{O}_4 + 2 \times \text{Mass of }\text{NO}_2}{x + 2y}
\]
The molar mass of \( \text{N}_2\text{O}_4 = 2 \times 14 + 4 \times 16 = 28 + 64 = 92 \) g/mol.
The molar mass of \( \text{NO}_2 = 14 + 2 \times 16 = 14 + 32 = 46 \) g of M.
At equilibrium, the total number of moles is \( n \) and the mole fraction of \( \text{N}_2\text{O}_4 \) is \( \alpha \). Then:
\[
\text{Moles of } \text{N}_2\text{O}_4 = \alpha n
\]
\[
\text{Moles of } \text{NO}_2 = 2(1 - \alpha)n
\]
The total number of moles is:
\[
\text{Total moles} = \alpha n + 2(1 - \alpha)n = \alpha n + 2n - 2\alpha n = 2n - \alpha n = n(2 - \alpha)
\]
The average molecular mass is:
\[
\text{Average molecular mass} = \frac{\alpha n \times 92 + 2(1 - \alpha)n \times 46}{n(2 - \alpha)} = \frac{92\alpha + 92 - 92\alpha}{2 - \alpha} = \frac{92}{2 - \alpha}
\]
The vapour density is:
\[
\text{Vapour density} = \frac{\text{Average molecular mass}}{\text{Relative molecular mass}} = \frac{92}{2 - \alpha} \times \frac{1}{28}
\]
Given that the vapour density is 25.67:
\[
25.67 = \frac{92}{2 - \alpha}
\]
\[
2 - \alpha = \frac{92}{25.67} = 3.59
\]
\[
\alpha = 2 - 3.59 = -1.59
\]
